"Internal Error ()\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/commits/main/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/commits/main/: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"id\":94}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRefinedRust: A Type System for High-Assurance Verification of Rust Programs (https://plv.mpi-sws.org/refinedrust/paper-refinedrust.pdf)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 25; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"id\":6}); Total lines: 1197\nL0@P0: RefinedRust: A Type System for High-Assurance Verification\nL1@P0: of Rust Programs\nL2@P0: LENNARD GÄHER, MPI-SWS, Germany\nL3@P0: MICHAEL SAMMLER, MPI-SWS, Germany\nL4@P0: RALF JUNG, ETH Zurich, Switzerland\nL5@P0: ROBBERT KREBBERS, Radboud University Nijmegen, Netherlands\nL6@P0: DEREK DREYER, MPI-SWS, Germany\nL7@P0: Rust is a modern systems programming language whose ownership-based type system statically guarantees\nL8@P0: memory safety, making it particularly well-suited to the domain of safety-critical systems. In recent years, a\nL9@P0: wellspring of automated deductive verification tools have emerged for establishing functional correctness of\nL10@P0: Rust code. However, none of the previous tools produce foundational proofs (machine-checkable in a general\u0002purpose proof assistant), and all of them are restricted to the safe fragment of Rust. This is a problem because\nL11@P0: the vast majority of Rust programs make use of unsafe code at critical points, such as in the implementation\nL12@P0: of widely-used APIs. We propose RefinedRust, a refinement type system—proven sound in the Coq proof\nL13@P0: assistant—with the goal of establishing foundational semi-automated functional correctness verification of\nL14@P0: both safe and unsafe Rust code. We have developed a prototype verification tool implementing RefinedRust.\nL15@P0: Our tool translates Rust code (with user annotations) into a model of Rust embedded in Coq, and then checks\nL16@P0: its adherence to the RefinedRust type system using separation logic automation in Coq. All proofs generated\nL17@P0: by RefinedRust are checked by the Coq proof assistant, so the automation and type system do not have to be\nL18@P0: trusted. We evaluate the effectiveness of RefinedRust by verifying a variant of Rust’s Vec implementation that\nL19@P0: involves intricate reasoning about unsafe pointer-manipulating code.\nL20@P0: CCS Concepts: • Theory of computation → Program verification; Separation logic.\nL21@P0: Additional Key Words and Phrases: separation logic, program verification, Rust, Iris\nL22@P0: ACM Reference Format:\nL23@P0: Lennard Gäher, Michael Sammler, Ralf Jung, Robbert Krebbers, and Derek Dreyer. 2024. RefinedRust: A Type\nL24@P0: System for High-Assurance Verification of Rust Programs. Proc. ACM Program. Lang. 8, PLDI, Article 192\nL25@P0: (June 2024), 25 pages. https://doi.org/10.1145/3656422\nL26@P0: 1 INTRODUCTION\nL27@P0: Rust [47] is a modern systems programming language that is seeing increasingly widespread\nL28@P0: adoption in industry as an essential tool for building more trustworthy systems code [2, 45]. One\nL29@P0: of Rust’s key selling points is that its core type system guarantees memory safety, thus ruling out\nL30@P0: common errors made by programmers in legacy systems programming languages like C and C++,\nL31@P0: without compromising on performance. Indeed, Rust’s memory safety guarantees are a primary\nL32@P0: factor driving its adoption in safety-critical systems like the Linux kernel [50].\nL33@P0: Authors’ addresses: Lennard Gäher, MPI-SWS, Saarland Informatics Campus, Germany, gaeher@mpi-sws.org; Michael\nL34@P0: Sammler, MPI-SWS, Saarland Informatics Campus, Germany, msammler@mpi-sws.org; Ralf Jung, ETH Zurich, Zürich,\nL35@P0: Switzerland, research@ralfj.de; Robbert Krebbers, Radboud University Nijmegen, Nijmegen, Netherlands, mail@\nL36@P0: robbertkrebbers.nl; Derek Dreyer, MPI-SWS, Saarland Informatics Campus, Germany, dreyer@mpi-sws.org.\nL37@P0: Permission to make digital or hard copies of part or all of this work for personal or classroom use is granted without fee\nL38@P0: provided that copies are not made or distributed for profit or commercial advantage and that copies bear this notice and\nL39@P0: the full citation on the first page. Copyrights for third-party components of this work must be honored. For all other uses,\nL40@P0: contact the owner/author(s).\nL41@P0: © 2024 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).\nL42@P0: ACM 2475-1421/2024/6-ART192\nL43@P0: https://doi.org/10.1145/3656422\nL44@P0-1: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 8, No. PLDI, Article 192. Publication date: June 2024.192:2 Lennard Gäher, Michael Sammler, Ralf Jung, Robbert Krebbers, and Derek Dreyer\nL45@P1: Of course, for safety-critical programs, it is not ultimately sufficient that they are memory-safe—\nL46@P1: we also want to establish that they do what they are supposed to do, i.e., that they satisfy functional\nL47@P1: correctness properties. Toward that end, there has emerged a wellspring of exciting research in\nL48@P1: recent years, leading to a range of new verification tools, such as Prusti [3], Creusot [9], Flux [28],\nL49@P1: and Aeneas [12]. These tools have made impressive strides forward, particularly in leveraging the\nL50@P1: expressive power of the Rust type system to simplify the task of verifying Rust programs.\nL51@P1: However, all the aforementioned verification tools share two key limitations. One is that, like\nL52@P1: most practical verification tools, they are standalone software artifacts, the implementations of\nL53@P1: which are increasingly complex and thus add significantly to the trusted computing base (TCB)\nL54@P1: of any verification conducted with them. The other limitation pertains to the handling of unsafe\nL55@P1: code. Although Rust is renowned for its safety guarantees, its type system is sometimes overly\nL56@P1: restrictive: there are certain systems programming idioms which cannot be implemented in safe\nL57@P1: Rust. As a result, many observably safe Rust APIs are implemented internally with sparing use of\nL58@P1: unsafe features of the language (such as raw pointer manipulations or unchecked type casts, which\nL59@P1: may result in undefined behavior). Yet none of the previous verification tools for Rust (see §7 for a\nL60@P1: comparison to GillianRust [55], which was developed concurrently with RefinedRust) support the\nL61@P1: verification of Rust APIs implemented with unsafe code.\nL62@P1: Ideally, we would like to develop technology for verifying Rust programs that avoids both of\nL63@P1: these limitations—i.e., that handles unsafe code, and that lowers the TCB by producing foundational\nL64@P1: proofs in a proof assistant such as Coq—while retaining support for automated verification.\nL65@P1: To that end, we present RefinedRust, a new approach to the foundational verification of\nL66@P1: Rust programs, based on refined ownership types [41]. RefinedRust is the first approach to Rust\nL67@P1: verification that simultaneously (1) handles real (surface) Rust code, (2) provides support for proof\nL68@P1: automation, both for safe and unsafe Rust code, and (3) outputs machine-checkable proofs for all\nL69@P1: verified code. We have implemented a prototype of RefinedRust in Coq. Its automation support is\nL70@P1: fairly basic compared to that of previous non-foundational Rust verification tools, but no previous\nL71@P1: tool (foundational or otherwise) supports any automation for verifying unsafe Rust code, and thus\nL72@P1: RefinedRust makes an important first step.\nL73@P1: As the name suggests, the starting point for RefinedRust is Sammler et al.’s earlier work on\nL74@P1: RefinedC [41], a system for verifying functional correctness of C programs that is both foundational\nL75@P1: and semi-automated. RefinedC achieves this goal by developing an extension of C’s type system\nL76@P1: with refinement and ownership types, which enable it to express rich functional specifications on\nL77@P1: the behavior of C code. RefinedC defines a semantic model of its refinement types in the separation\nL78@P1: logic Iris [21, 19, 25, 20, 26, 44], so that the soundness of RefinedC type checking is established\nL79@P1: foundationally in Coq. Moreover, RefinedC’s typing rules are expressed in a fragment of Iris called\nL80@P1: Lithium [40], which is carefully designed to admit efficient proof search without backtracking; as a\nL81@P1: result, RefinedC type checking can be performed with a (relatively) high degree of automation.\nL82@P1: At a high level, the idea behind RefinedRust is to take RefinedC’s approach of refined ownership\nL83@P1: types and figure out how to make it work for Rust. The most obvious challenge in doing so is\nL84@P1: developing useful refinement types (and typing rules) to automate reasoning about Rust’s most\nL85@P1: distinctive feature: its reference types, along with their attendant notions of lifetimes and borrowing.\nL86@P1: Towards that end, we take inspiration from RustBelt [18], leveraging its lifetime logic.\nL87@P1: But of course the devil is in the details. In developing RefinedRust, we had to overcome a number\nL88@P1: of technical challenges, related to: (1) bridging the gap between Rust and the RustBelt model, and\nL89@P1: (2) adapting RefinedC’s refinement type system to handle Rust types.\nL90@P1: Challenge #1: Bridging the gap between Rust and RustBelt. As explained above, RefinedRust\nL91@P1: achieves foundational verification by giving a semantic model of Rust types as predicates in the\nL92@P1-2: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 8, No. PLDI, Article 192. Publication date: June 2024.RefinedRust: A Type System for High-Assurance Verification of Rust Programs 192:3\nL93@P2: Iris separation logic [48]. This semantic model is inspired closely by that of RustBelt, but RustBelt\nL94@P2: employs an idealized formalization of Rust called 𝜆Rust. In order to account for real Rust code, we\nL95@P2: had to overcome two key gaps between RustBelt/𝜆Rust and Rust.\nL96@P2: First of all, RustBelt makes no attempt to capture the notion of “places” in the Rust language (also\nL97@P2: known as “lvalues” in C). Places occur on the left-hand side of assignments and as the operands of\nL98@P2: the “address of” operator &; for instance, in &x.f, the expression x.f denotes the place in memory\nL99@P2: where the f field of variable x is stored. In RustBelt, to port a Rust program to 𝜆Rust, one must\nL100@P2: replace uses of general places by a few simple cases that the RustBelt type system can handle. This\nL101@P2: requires manual effort and fails to properly reflect the structure of the Rust source code.\nL102@P2: Secondly, 𝜆Rust does not accurately reflect all aspects of Rust code. For instance, integers in 𝜆Rust\nL103@P2: are unbounded (as opposed to Rust’s real semantics with integer overflows), and 𝜆Rust does not\nL104@P2: accurately reflect how data is represented in memory, especially for compound types like structs.\nL105@P2: RefinedRust lifts both of these limitations. As a more realistic operational semantics suitable\nL106@P2: for functional verification of unsafe code, RefinedRust introduces Radium (based on RefinedC’s\nL107@P2: Caesium model for C [41]). Hence, programs verified in RefinedRust are proven to correctly deal\nL108@P2: with intricacies such as integer overflows. To ensure correctness independent of the concrete data\nL109@P2: layout, RefinedRust parameterizes its verification by an arbitrary “layout algorithm” (§4). And to\nL110@P2: properly account for the role places play in Rust, RefinedRust’s type system introduces place types.\nL111@P2: This aligns RefinedRust sufficiently well with the Rust type checker that we can automatically\nL112@P2: translate Rust code into Radium and type check the result with RefinedRust.\nL113@P2: Challenge #2: Extending RefinedC with refinement types for Rust’s mutable references.\nL114@P2: The basic structure of RefinedRust is modeled after that of RefinedC: it layers a refinement type\nL115@P2: system on top of Rust’s type system, and then expresses its refinement type checking rules in the\nL116@P2: Lithium fragment of Iris to make it amenable to proof automation. However, Rust is a very different\nL117@P2: language from C, so “porting RefinedC to Rust” is far from straightforward. In particular, C only\nL118@P2: has a weak type system that describes the layout of values in memory, but does not give strong\nL119@P2: guarantees or provide many mechanisms for abstraction. RefinedC therefore introduces its own\nL120@P2: type system with a Rust-inspired notion of exclusive ownership. However, the Rust type system\nL121@P2: goes well beyond exclusive ownership, using borrowing to grant temporary access to data without\nL122@P2: full ownership transfer. Borrowing in Rust is expressed via reference types: shared references &T for\nL123@P2: immutable borrowing, and mutable references &mut T for mutable borrowing.\nL124@P2: Extending the RefinedC type system to handle Rust’s shared references is fairly straightforward,\nL125@P2: but mutable references constitute a major challenge. To explain why, we have to briefly consider\nL126@P2: how RefinedC represents a variable with a known value: 42 @ inti32 is the (singleton) type of an\nL127@P2: integer with value 42. Similarly, &own(42 @ inti32) is the type of an exclusively owned pointer that\nL128@P2: points to an integer with value 42. Note how the owned pointer type entirely wraps the integer type,\nL129@P2: including its value. If the program changes the value stored behind that pointer to 57, RefinedC uses\nL130@P2: a “strong update” (i.e., type-changing update) to enable the pointer type to be changed accordingly\nL131@P2: to &own(57 @ inti32). Such a strong update is sound precisely because the pointer is exclusively\nL132@P2: owned: there is no risk that any other part of the program could have a different view on this data\nL133@P2: that would be in conflict with a strong update.\nL134@P2: In contrast, a mutable reference in Rust is not exclusively owned—rather, it is borrowed from\nL135@P2: somewhere, and once the borrow expires, the original owner will want to use that data again at its\nL136@P2: original type. This problem is an instance of the common pattern that when a reference type allows\nL137@P2: for shared state (in this case, state that is shared between the borrower and the original owner), the\nL138@P2: type must be invariant. As a result, Rust’s mutable references do not allow strong updates, and the\nL139@P2: RefinedC strategy for precisely tracking the values stored in them no longer works.\nL140@P2-3: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 8, No. PLDI, Article 192. Publication date: June 2024.192:4 Lennard Gäher, Michael Sammler, Ralf Jung, Robbert Krebbers, and Derek Dreyer\nL141@P3: In RefinedRust, we therefore keep the value “outside” of the mutable reference: a mutable\nL142@P3: reference that points to an integer with value 42 would, roughly, have type 42 @ &mutinti32. This\nL143@P3: lets us change the value without performing a strong update on the mutable reference type. However,\nL144@P3: this does not solve the issue that eventually, the lifetime of the reference will expire, and then the\nL145@P3: information about the new value stored behind that reference has to be propagated back to its\nL146@P3: original owner. To this end, RefinedRust introduces borrow names, which are inspired by RustHorn’s\nL147@P3: prophecy variables [32]: the full type of a mutable reference takes the shape (42,𝛾) @ &mutinti32\nL148@P3: where 42 is the current value, and 𝛾 is a borrow name that lets the original owner incorporate\nL149@P3: changes to that value into their own proof (§2.2). This change represents a fundamental departure\nL150@P3: from RefinedC in terms of how type constructors interact with refinements, requiring a redesign of\nL151@P3: large parts of the type system and a non-trivial extension to RustBelt’s lifetime logic.\nL152@P3: Contributions. We present RefinedRust, a new foundational approach for verifying functional\nL153@P3: correctness of safe and unsafe Rust programs. We make the following conceptual contributions:\nL154@P3: • We show how to handle Rust’s mutable references in a refinement type system, using the\nL155@P3: novel mechanism of borrow names to link the value of a mutable reference with the value of\nL156@P3: the borrowed place (§2.2, §2.3).\nL157@P3: • To support the borrowing patterns that appear when verifying actual Rust code, we build a\nL158@P3: place type system for RefinedRust including novel types that enable Rust-specific reasoning\nL159@P3: about borrowed places and types with invariants (§3.2, §5.2, §5.3). For our soundness proof\nL160@P3: of the type system, we have extended RustBelt’s lifetime logic with a new kind of borrows\nL161@P3: (described in the supplementary material [11]).\nL162@P3: • To verify Rust’s polymorphic functions and to support layout-generic verification, we develop\nL163@P3: a semantics which parameterizes the code with type parameters and a layout algorithm. The\nL164@P3: verification then happens generically in the layout algorithm and type parameters (§4.1).\nL165@P3: Additionally, we provide an implementation of RefinedRust with the following components:\nL166@P3: • The RefinedRust type system, a type system for Rust that combines refined ownership types\nL167@P3: with a semantic model of Rust types inspired by RustBelt (§5).\nL168@P3: • Radium, a formalization of Rust, based on RefinedC’s Caesium operational semantics (§4).\nL169@P3: • A type checker for RefinedRust based on RefinedC’s Lithium proof engine in Coq, and a\nL170@P3: frontend that translates Rust code to Radium in Coq by leveraging the Rust compiler (§6).\nL171@P3: • We evaluate RefinedRust on a version of the Rust standard library’s Vec vector implementation\nL172@P3: (§3, §6). The code has been simplified for engineering reasons, but it still captures many of\nL173@P3: the intricate challenges of working with pointer-manipulating unsafe Rust code.\nL174@P3: The RefinedRust type system and the RefinedRust type checker are mechanized in Coq using\nL175@P3: Iris. §5.4 describes the high-level soundness result—the technical details, including RefinedRust’s\nL176@P3: extensions to RustBelt’s lifetime logic, can be found in the supplementary material [11].\nL177@P3: Non-goals and limitations. The RefinedRust prototype produces proofs directly in Coq, which\nL178@P3: means that its implementation does not need to be trusted. This also limits the approaches that we\nL179@P3: can use for automation (e.g., SMT solvers as trusted oracles) compared to other verification tools\nL180@P3: for Rust, such as Creusot [9] and Prusti [3]. While RefinedRust closes some gaps between RustBelt\nL181@P3: and real Rust, there are still aspects of the Rust semantics that we do not yet account for, such as\nL182@P3: some details of Rust’s layouting of structs and enums (e.g., the niche optimizations [4]), some of\nL183@P3: Rust’s validity invariants (e.g., that booleans values are always 0 or 1), pointer-integer casts, and the\nL184@P3: aliasing model [17] (which remains a topic of active research). A complete and precise specification\nL185@P3: of the operational semantics of Rust does not exist yet. RefinedRust does not support some more\nL186@P3: advanced features of Rust such as concurrency, recursive types, traits, closures, and unsized types.\nL187@P3-4: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 8, No. PLDI, Article 192. Publication date: June 2024.RefinedRust: A Type System for High-Assurance Verification of Rust Programs 192:5\nL188@P4: 1 #[rr::params(\"x\" : \"Z\")]\nL189@P4: 2 #[rr::args(\"#x\")]\nL190@P4: 3 #[rr::requires(\"x + 42 ∈ i32\")]\nL191@P4: 4 #[rr::returns(\"#(x + 42)\")]\nL192@P4: 5 fn box_add_42(mut r: Box<i32>) -> Box<i32>\nL193@P4: 6 { *r += 42; r }\nL194@P4: Fig. 1. Function that adds 42 to an integer stored\nL195@P4: in a box.\nL196@P4: 1 #[rr::params(\"x\" : \"Z\", \"𝛾\" : \"gname\")]\nL197@P4: 2 #[rr::args(\"(#x, 𝛾)\")]\nL198@P4: 3 #[rr::requires(\"x + 42 ∈ i32\")]\nL199@P4: 4 #[rr::ensures(#iris \"Res 𝛾 (x + 42)\")]\nL200@P4: 5 fn mut_ref_add_42(r: &mut i32)\nL201@P4: 6 { *r += 42 }\nL202@P4: Fig. 2. Function that adds 42 to an integer stored\nL203@P4: in a mutable reference.\nL204@P4: 2 AN INTRODUCTION TO REFINEDRUST\nL205@P4: We outline the basic principles of RefinedRust using our prototype implementation (described in\nL206@P4: §6). Then we introduce our notion of refined ownership types (§2.1), and their combination with\nL207@P4: Rust’s mutable references (§2.2 and §2.3). Finally, we show our handling of unsafe functions (§2.4).\nL208@P4: 2.1 Refinement Types\nL209@P4: Inspired by RefinedC, RefinedRust enables functional correctness verification through refinement\nL210@P4: types. Let us explain refinement types in RefinedRust by considering the function box_add_42, shown\nL211@P4: in Figure 1. It takes an argument r of type Box<i32>, where Box<T> is Rust’s type for an owned pointer\nL212@P4: to a value of type T. The function adds 42 to the value stored in r, and then returns r to the caller.\nL213@P4: The type Box<T> has full ownership of the memory the pointer refers to, which means there cannot\nL214@P4: be other pointers to the memory. It is thus necessary that r is returned to give ownership of the\nL215@P4: memory back to the caller, otherwise Rust would drop the box and free the memory.\nL216@P4: In RefinedRust’s type system, all types come with a notion of mathematical values that they are\nL217@P4: refined by. Consider the specification for box_add_42 in Figure 1. Here, refinement type information\nL218@P4: is given via Rust attributes #[rr::...]. First, the params attribute introduces a specification variable x\nL219@P4: of the mathematical (unbounded) integer type Z. The args attribute then links this variable to the\nL220@P4: function argument r of Rust type Box<i32>, by stating that r is refined by the mathematical integer x.\nL221@P4: (The injection # is discussed in §2.3.) The requires attribute on line 3 specifies the precondition\nL222@P4: that x + 42—the result of the addition performed by the function—must be in the value range\nL223@P4: representable by the type i32. The precondition ensures that the addition does not overflow,\nL224@P4: which would trigger a panic and abort program execution. The precondition is necessary because\nL225@P4: RefinedRust also verifies that no panics occur, similar to other Rust verification tools [3, 9]. Finally,\nL226@P4: the returns attribute specifies the mathematical value x + 42 of the box returned by box_add_42.\nL227@P4: 2.2 Mutable References\nL228@P4: The function mut_ref_add_42 in Figure 2 is a more idiomatic version of the previous example. It uses\nL229@P4: Rust’s mutable reference type &mut T instead of a Box<T> to avoid returning the box. Like Box<T>, a\nL230@P4: mutable reference &mut T asserts exclusive ownership of its memory, and thus allows mutating it\nL231@P4: (e.g., by adding 42). However, this exclusive ownership is limited in time: a mutable reference has a\nL232@P4: lifetime, and only borrows the referenced memory for this lifetime. Once the function returns, the\nL233@P4: lifetime is over and the caller regains ownership of the memory. To illustrate this point, consider:\nL234@P4: let mut z = 1; mut_ref_add_42(&mut z); assert!(z == 43);\nL235@P4: The use of z in the assert! implicitly ends the lifetime of the reference passed to mut_ref_add_42.\nL236@P4: Proving that the assert! succeeds requires knowing that z is indeed incremented by 42. However,\nL237@P4: there is no explicit flow of values from mut_ref_add_42 to the assert! since mut_ref_add_42 returns a\nL238@P4: unit value. Unlike the previous example, we thus cannot use the returns attribute to specify the\nL239@P4: increment of z, and need another way to specify the side-effect of incrementing z.\nL240@P4-5: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 8, No. PLDI, Article 192. Publication date: June 2024.192:6 Lennard Gäher, Michael Sammler, Ralf Jung, Robbert Krebbers, and Derek Dreyer\nL241@P5: To reason about mutable references, RefinedRust uses the notion of borrow names (inspired by\nL242@P5: RustHorn’s prophecy variables [32]). The mathematical value of a mutable reference &mut T is a\nL243@P5: pair (x,𝛾), where x is the current mathematical value of type T and the borrow name 𝛾 is used to\nL244@P5: communicate the final value of the reference. In our example, &mut z will create a reference with\nL245@P5: mathematical value (1,𝛾) for some fresh 𝛾, and the value of z changes from 1 to ∗𝛾. In other words,\nL246@P5: the value “moves” from z to the new reference, and 𝛾 ties the reference to its referent. This reference\nL247@P5: is then passed to mut_ref_add_42 (instantiating x with 1). The ensures clause (i.e., postcondition) of\nL248@P5: that function is a resolution Res 𝛾 (x + 42), which states that the final value of the reference with\nL249@P5: borrow name 𝛾 is x + 42 (the #iris annotation specifies that the postcondition is a separation logic\nL250@P5: assertion). This resolution lets RefinedRust automatically resolve the mathematical value of z from\nL251@P5: ∗𝛾 to 43. Thus, it can prove that the assert! succeeds.\nL252@P5: 2.3 Reborrows\nL253@P5: Now that we have seen the basics of refinement types and borrows in RefinedRust, let us turn\nL254@P5: to a more advanced use case of mutable references: the method get_mut for creating a mutable\nL255@P5: reference to an element of a Rust vector. In this section, we use get_mut to explain the concept\nL256@P5: of reborrowing. In §2.4, we will verify the implementation of get_mut based on the unsafe helper\nL257@P5: function get_unchecked_mut, which we in turn verify in §3. The signature of get_mut is:\nL258@P5: fn Vec::get_mut<'a>(&'a mut self, idx: usize) -> Option<&'a mut T>\nL259@P5: It takes a mutable reference to the vector self as well as an index idx. It checks if idx is within\nL260@P5: bounds of the vector, and if so, returns a reference to that element of the vector (else it returns None).\nL261@P5: The returned reference can be used by the caller to modify this element of the vector, and Rust’s\nL262@P5: borrow checker makes sure that the vector is inaccessible as long as the reference is in use. This is\nL263@P5: especially clear when considering the lifetimes of the references in the type signature: the vector\nL264@P5: gets a reference of lifetime 'a, and the returned reference to the element has the same lifetime 'a.\nL265@P5: This means that, as long as the returned reference is in use, the reference passed as argument is also\nL266@P5: in use. As such, get_mut is an example of a reborrow function common in Rust, taking a reference as\nL267@P5: an argument and then providing a “view” into that reference in the return value.\nL268@P5: Figure 3 shows a simple client of get_mut. It creates a vector x containing 100, 200, and 300, and\nL269@P5: uses x.get_mut(1) to get a mutable reference xr to the element at index 1. The function unwrap takes\nL270@P5: an Option<T> and returns t if the input is Some(t), and panics if it is None. Since 1 is within range of\nL271@P5: the vector x, such a panic cannot happen. The first assert! checks that xr indeed refers to 200. After\nL272@P5: updating the value of xr to 42, the second assert! checks that the write to rx updated the vector x as\nL273@P5: expected. Importantly, in the second assert!, the vector is accessed through x again, which means\nL274@P5: that the lifetime 'a of the reference returned by get_mut ends and rx cannot be used anymore.\nL275@P5: Let us look at the specification for get_mut in Figure 3 to see how it enables the verification of the\nL276@P5: assertions in get_mut_client. The first argument self has the Rust type &mut Vec<T>, so it is refined by\nL277@P5: a list xs of (borrowable) mathematical values for T,\nL278@P5: 1\nL279@P5: together with a borrow name 𝛾 for the mutable\nL280@P5: reference. The second argument idx is refined by a mathematical integer i : Z. The remaining\nL281@P5: clauses specify the postcondition: exists declares a mathematical variable that is returned by the\nL282@P5: function (akin to an existential quantifier in the postcondition). The returns clause specifies the\nL283@P5: real return value: if the index i is within bounds of the vector, get_mut returns Some containing a\nL284@P5: reference to the i-th element of xs with the fresh borrow name 𝛾i, otherwise it returns None. Here,\nL285@P5: the syntax xs !!! i indicates list indexing.\nL286@P5: The most interesting part of this specification is the ensures clause: if i is out of bounds, the\nL287@P5: caller receives Res 𝛾 xs, stating that the vector is unchanged. (Recall that 𝛾 is the borrow name for\nL288@P5: 1{math_type T} denotes the type of mathematical values for T. We come back to the bor wrapper shortly.\nL289@P5-6: Proc. ACM Program. Lang., Vol. 8, No. PLDI, Article 192. Publication date: June 2024.RefinedRust: A Type System for High-Assurance Verification of Rust Programs 192:7\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArtifact for \"RefinedRust: A Type System for High-Assurance Verification of Rust Programs\" | Zenodo (https://zenodo.org/doi/10.5281/zenodo.10800313)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"id\":9}); Redirected to URL: 